WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.536
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.836 --> 00:00:09.269
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.569 --> 00:00:16.575
Unit fourteen: Advanced Participle Clauses. Today you will combine clauses with perfect and passive participles.

4
00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:19.783
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:23.920
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.783 --> 00:00:25.889
Having done.

7
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:30.253
Having done means a perfect participle for an earlier action.

8
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:32.919
Having done his best, he rested.

9
00:00:33.517 --> 00:00:35.797
Your turn. Say: Having done.

10
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:39.774
Having done.

11
00:00:40.517 --> 00:00:42.505
Being + past participle.

12
00:00:43.517 --> 00:00:47.258
Being + past participle means a passive participle clause.

13
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.652
Being told to wait, he sat.

14
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.692
Your turn. Say: Being + past participle.

15
00:00:56.533 --> 00:00:58.510
Being + past participle.

16
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.681
Perfect participle.

17
00:01:01.683 --> 00:01:04.855
Perfect participle means having + past participle.

18
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.020
A perfect participle.

19
00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:10.548
Your turn. Say: Perfect participle.

20
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.109
Perfect participle.

21
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.606
Passive participle clause.

22
00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:22.741
Passive participle clause means a clause with a passive meaning.

23
00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:25.477
A passive participle clause.

24
00:01:26.083 --> 00:01:29.897
Your turn. Say: Passive participle clause.

25
00:01:32.733 --> 00:01:34.744
Passive participle clause.

26
00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:36.937
Reduced clause.

27
00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:41.610
Reduced clause means a clause shortened with a participle.

28
00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:43.763
A reduced clause.

29
00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:47.165
Your turn. Say: Reduced clause.

30
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.547
Reduced clause.

31
00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:53.621
Formal economy.

32
00:01:54.617 --> 00:01:58.416
Formal economy means saving words in formal style.

33
00:01:59.017 --> 00:02:00.238
Formal economy.

34
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:04.118
Your turn. Say: Formal economy.

35
00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:08.989
Formal economy.

36
00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.146
Concise structure.

37
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.450
Concise structure means a short, clear structure.

38
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.690
A concise structure.

39
00:02:18.283 --> 00:02:21.272
Your turn. Say: Concise structure.

40
00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:25.552
Concise structure.

41
00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:27.805
Academic style.

42
00:02:28.817 --> 00:02:32.233
Academic style means the style of research papers.

43
00:02:32.817 --> 00:02:34.491
An academic style.

44
00:02:35.083 --> 00:02:38.397
Your turn. Say: Academic style.

45
00:02:41.233 --> 00:02:42.792
Academic style.

46
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.204
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:52.046
Visal, how do we shorten Because she had been warned, she left early?

48
00:02:53.150 --> 00:03:04.838
Having been warned, she left early. We combine the perfect and the passive: having been + past participle. It explains the reason and the earlier time.

49
00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:07.543
Can we use being alone?

50
00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:19.641
Yes. Being tired, he went to bed means Because he was tired. Being asked to wait, he sat down uses the passive to show he received the request.

51
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.007
What are the rules?

52
00:03:23.117 --> 00:03:35.536
The subject of both clauses must be the same. A negative goes first: Not having studied, he failed. Without a common subject, the clause dangles and confuses the reader.

53
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:38.073
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:38.917 --> 00:03:45.839
Grammar focus: Advanced participle clause: having plus participle and being plus participle, main clause.

55
00:03:46.139 --> 00:03:47.279
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.983 --> 00:03:50.262
Having been warned, she left early.

57
00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:52.824
Being tired, he went to bed.

58
00:03:53.717 --> 00:03:56.146
Not having studied, he failed the test.

59
00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:05.139
Use having + participle for an earlier action and being + participle for a passive reason. Keep the same subject in both clauses.

60
00:04:05.883 --> 00:04:09.685
Be careful! Don't say: Having been tired, he slept.

61
00:04:11.633 --> 00:04:13.981
Say: Being tired, he slept.

62
00:04:14.281 --> 00:04:15.909
Use being for a state.

63
00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.561
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:20.367 --> 00:04:21.472
Reading the Warning.

65
00:04:21.772 --> 00:04:25.339
Having been warned about the storm, the sailors returned to port.

66
00:04:26.333 --> 00:04:28.786
Being experienced, the captain stayed calm.

67
00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.519
Not having prepared, the crew had little time.

68
00:04:33.533 --> 00:04:36.241
Such clauses are typical of academic style.

69
00:04:37.233 --> 00:04:40.185
They save words and show the logic between ideas.

70
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:45.313
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:47.697
blank warned, she left.

72
00:04:47.997 --> 00:04:51.650
A, Having been. B, Been having. C, Having.

73
00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.165
The answer is A: Having been.

74
00:04:58.317 --> 00:05:00.270
Which means 'Because he was tired'?

75
00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:04.513
A, To tire. B, Being tired. C, Having tired.

76
00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:10.496
The answer is B: Being tired.

77
00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:14.381
A negative participle clause begins with blank.

78
00:05:14.681 --> 00:05:17.800
A, not. B, never. C, no.

79
00:05:21.367 --> 00:05:23.122
The answer is A: not.

80
00:05:24.283 --> 00:05:26.782
The subject of both clauses must be blank.

81
00:05:27.082 --> 00:05:30.840
A, plural. B, the same. C, different.

82
00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.400
The answer is B: the same.

83
00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:39.875
According to the reading, who stayed calm?

84
00:05:40.175 --> 00:05:44.153
A, the captain. B, the crew. C, the sailors.

85
00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.653
The answer is A: the captain.

86
00:05:50.867 --> 00:05:54.053
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:54.917 --> 00:05:59.842
Rewrite a two-clause sentence using an advanced participle clause.

88
00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.208
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:10.508 --> 00:06:21.511
Because he had finished the report, he went home. Having finished the report, he went home. The participle clause is shorter, and it keeps the same subject, he.

90
00:06:21.811 --> 00:06:23.567
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:32.954
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and advanced participle clause: having plus participle and being plus participle, main clause.

92
00:06:33.254 --> 00:06:38.936
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

93
00:06:39.236 --> 00:06:41.982
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:42.282 --> 00:06:45.514
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

